; Copy this file to secrets.local.ini to override the fleet API key without
; putting the new value in git.
;
;     cp secrets.local.ini.example secrets.local.ini
;
; You do not need this for the demo fleet. The demo key is in platformio.ini as
; custom_fleet_api_key, in plain sight, because it guards a service that holds
; nothing confidential and permits nothing destructive.
;
; You DO need it the moment that stops being true - a fleet with real nodes in
; real places, or a service anyone could abuse by filing reports into it. At
; that point the key becomes a secret, and a secret in platformio.ini is a
; secret in every clone and in every copy of the history from the commit that
; added it. Rotating the key does not remove it from the past; only never
; committing the new one keeps it out.
;
; secrets.local.ini is gitignored. This example file is not, because it is the
; documentation - it is what tells the next person the mechanism exists.
;
; Read by scripts/version_flags.py at the start of every build. Precedence,
; highest first:
;
;   1. the LORA_FLEET_API_KEY environment variable   (a one-off build)
;   2. this file                                     (a real fleet)
;   3. custom_fleet_api_key in platformio.ini        (the demo fleet)
;
; The build says which value it used and how long it is:
;
;     version_flags: fleet API key compiled in (64 chars)
;
; and says so plainly when there is none, in which case each node has to be
; given one over the cable with `net set key <key>`. See docs/COMMISSIONING.md.
;
; Two things worth knowing either way:
;
;   * The value must match FLEET__DEVICEAPIKEY in the service's environment
;     exactly. A wrong key and no key produce the same 401.
;   * An image built with a key HAS the key in it. Once the key is a secret, so
;     is every binary built from it - including anything uploaded to the fleet
;     service as a firmware release.

LORA_FLEET_API_KEY = paste-the-value-of-FLEET__DEVICEAPIKEY-here
